1
00:00:00,450 --> 00:00:01,784
A crisp lettuce leaf.

2
00:00:01,784 --> 00:00:02,762
A wooden door.

3
00:00:02,762 --> 00:00:03,784
A cotton shirt.

4
00:00:04,184 --> 00:00:07,550
All three get their strength
from the walls of plant cells.

5
00:00:08,000 --> 00:00:09,922
Aarav watched the college canteen.

6
00:00:09,922 --> 00:00:13,836
Food is cooked in the kitchen,
packed at the counter, and sent out.

7
00:00:13,836 --> 00:00:16,211
He asked: what keeps a plant cell firm?

8
00:00:16,211 --> 00:00:19,561
And how does a cell make, pack
and send out its proteins?

9
00:00:19,911 --> 00:00:20,811
Two answers.

10
00:00:20,590 --> 00:00:22,683
A rigid cell wall keeps
the plant cell firm.

11
00:00:22,683 --> 00:00:25,875
And a set of connected membranes,
the endomembrane system, makes, packs

12
00:00:25,875 --> 00:00:27,276
and ships the cell's products.

13
00:00:27,776 --> 00:00:32,175
By the end, you will know the cell wall
and its layers, and the endoplasmic

14
00:00:32,175 --> 00:00:35,192
reticulum, Golgi apparatus,
lysosomes and vacuoles.

15
00:00:35,842 --> 00:00:37,192
First, the cell wall.

16
00:00:37,542 --> 00:00:42,123
The cell wall is a non living, rigid
layer outside the plasma membrane.

17
00:00:42,123 --> 00:00:44,345
It is found in plants and fungi.

18
00:00:44,795 --> 00:00:48,936
It gives the cell its shape, and
protects it from damage and infection.

19
00:00:48,936 --> 00:00:52,794
It helps cells interact, and it
keeps out large unwanted molecules.

20
00:00:52,794 --> 00:00:56,926
In algae, the wall is made of cellulose,
galactans, mannans and minerals

21
00:00:56,926 --> 00:00:58,324
like calcium carbonate.

22
00:00:58,324 --> 00:01:02,438
In other plants, it is cellulose,
hemicellulose, pectins and proteins.

23
00:01:03,088 --> 00:01:05,711
Let's zoom into the walls of plant cells.

24
00:01:06,061 --> 00:01:09,008
Here are neighbouring
plant cells, side by side.

25
00:01:09,008 --> 00:01:12,010
Between them is a layer
called the middle lamella.

26
00:01:12,010 --> 00:01:15,759
It is mainly calcium pectate,
and it glues the cells together.

27
00:01:16,259 --> 00:01:17,159
Zoom in.

28
00:01:16,940 --> 00:01:19,903
A young cell has a primary
wall, which can grow.

29
00:01:19,903 --> 00:01:23,910
As the cell matures, a secondary
wall forms on the inner side, towards

30
00:01:23,910 --> 00:01:24,812
the membrane.

31
00:01:25,312 --> 00:01:26,212
Zoom again.

32
00:01:26,185 --> 00:01:29,399
Tiny channels cross the
wall and the middle lamella.

33
00:01:29,399 --> 00:01:30,945
These are plasmodesmata.

34
00:01:31,445 --> 00:01:35,514
Plasmodesmata connect the cytoplasm
of neighbouring cells, so the cells

35
00:01:35,514 --> 00:01:36,867
can share materials.

36
00:01:37,517 --> 00:01:41,028
Now inside the cell, the
endomembrane system, step by step.

37
00:01:41,378 --> 00:01:42,278
Step one.

38
00:01:42,095 --> 00:01:46,006
The endomembrane system is a group of
membrane bound organelles whose work

39
00:01:46,006 --> 00:01:46,986
is coordinated.

40
00:01:46,986 --> 00:01:50,836
It includes the endoplasmic reticulum,
the Golgi complex, lysosomes and

41
00:01:50,836 --> 00:01:51,736
vacuoles.

42
00:01:52,251 --> 00:01:53,151
Step two.

43
00:01:52,851 --> 00:01:56,035
Mitochondria, chloroplasts and
peroxisomes are not part of it, because

44
00:01:56,035 --> 00:01:58,026
their work is not coordinated with these.

45
00:01:58,776 --> 00:01:59,734
Step three.

46
00:01:59,734 --> 00:02:04,066
The endoplasmic reticulum, or
ER, is a network of tiny tubes.

47
00:02:04,066 --> 00:02:08,741
It splits the inside of the cell
into two spaces: the lumen inside the

48
00:02:08,741 --> 00:02:11,198
ER, and the cytoplasm outside it.

49
00:02:11,948 --> 00:02:12,848
Step four.

50
00:02:12,682 --> 00:02:15,245
ER with ribosomes on
its surface is rough ER.

51
00:02:15,245 --> 00:02:19,031
It is common in cells that make and
secrete proteins, and it joins the

52
00:02:19,031 --> 00:02:20,759
outer membrane of the nucleus.

53
00:02:21,509 --> 00:02:22,409
Step five.

54
00:02:22,161 --> 00:02:23,893
ER without ribosomes is smooth ER.

55
00:02:23,893 --> 00:02:25,864
It is the main site for making lipids.

56
00:02:25,864 --> 00:02:28,053
In animal cells, it
makes steroid hormones.

57
00:02:28,803 --> 00:02:29,703
Remember this.

58
00:02:29,533 --> 00:02:31,347
ER makes, Golgi packs, vesicles ship.

59
00:02:31,947 --> 00:02:33,316
Now the Golgi apparatus.

60
00:02:33,666 --> 00:02:37,904
Camillo Golgi first saw it in 1898,
as darkly stained structures near the

61
00:02:37,904 --> 00:02:38,804
nucleus.

62
00:02:38,561 --> 00:02:43,086
It is a stack of flat, disc shaped
sacs, called cisternae, each about zero

63
00:02:43,086 --> 00:02:45,316
point five to one micrometre across.

64
00:02:45,766 --> 00:02:47,540
The stack has two faces.

65
00:02:47,540 --> 00:02:50,563
The cis face, or forming face, is convex.

66
00:02:50,563 --> 00:02:53,788
The trans face, or
maturing face, is concave.

67
00:02:54,238 --> 00:02:57,432
Vesicles from the ER join the cis
face, and their contents move towards

68
00:02:57,432 --> 00:02:58,332
the trans face.

69
00:02:58,273 --> 00:03:00,063
Along the way, proteins are changed.

70
00:03:00,063 --> 00:03:03,278
The Golgi is an important site for
making glycoproteins and glycolipids.

71
00:03:03,928 --> 00:03:07,237
Two more members of the
system: lysosomes and vacuoles.

72
00:03:07,587 --> 00:03:10,728
Lysosomes are small bags formed
by packing in the Golgi apparatus.

73
00:03:10,728 --> 00:03:14,137
They are full of digestive enzymes,
called hydrolases, that work best in

74
00:03:14,137 --> 00:03:15,037
acid.

75
00:03:15,004 --> 00:03:16,935
These enzymes, lipases, proteases and
carbohydrases, can digest carbohydrates,

76
00:03:16,935 --> 00:03:17,883
proteins, lipids and nucleic acids.

77
00:03:18,333 --> 00:03:22,787
A vacuole is a space bound by a
single membrane, the tonoplast.

78
00:03:22,787 --> 00:03:25,119
It holds water, sap and waste.

79
00:03:25,119 --> 00:03:29,964
In plant cells, vacuoles can fill
up to ninety per cent of the cell.

80
00:03:30,614 --> 00:03:32,433
Vacuoles do more than store.

81
00:03:32,783 --> 00:03:37,615
In plants, the tonoplast moves ions into
the vacuole against the concentration

82
00:03:37,615 --> 00:03:38,515
gradient.

83
00:03:38,384 --> 00:03:43,428
So their concentration inside the vacuole
is much higher than in the cytoplasm.

84
00:03:43,878 --> 00:03:46,940
In Amoeba, a contractile vacuole
removes extra water and waste.

85
00:03:46,940 --> 00:03:50,502
And in protists, food vacuoles form
when the cell swallows food particles.

86
00:03:51,102 --> 00:03:52,002
Your turn.

87
00:03:51,820 --> 00:03:55,601
Which one is not part of the endomembrane
system: the Golgi complex, or

88
00:03:55,601 --> 00:03:56,650
the mitochondrion?

89
00:03:56,650 --> 00:03:57,706
Pause and decide.

90
00:04:00,906 --> 00:04:01,970
The mitochondrion!

91
00:04:01,970 --> 00:04:05,921
Its work is not coordinated with the
ER, Golgi, lysosomes and vacuoles.

92
00:04:06,571 --> 00:04:08,106
Now, a worked example.

93
00:04:08,106 --> 00:04:10,305
Follow one protein out of the cell.

94
00:04:10,655 --> 00:04:11,555
Stage one.

95
00:04:11,400 --> 00:04:15,150
Ribosomes on the rough ER make
the protein, and it enters the ER.

96
00:04:15,600 --> 00:04:16,500
Stage two.

97
00:04:16,248 --> 00:04:18,790
A vesicle carries it to
the cis face of the Golgi.

98
00:04:18,790 --> 00:04:20,511
There, it is changed and packed.

99
00:04:20,961 --> 00:04:21,880
Stage three.

100
00:04:21,880 --> 00:04:25,242
It leaves from the trans
face, inside a new vesicle.

101
00:04:25,692 --> 00:04:30,278
The vesicle joins the plasma membrane,
and the protein is sent out of the

102
00:04:30,278 --> 00:04:31,178
cell.

103
00:04:31,482 --> 00:04:32,945
Now, the exam trap.

104
00:04:33,295 --> 00:04:36,325
Many students think mitochondria and
chloroplasts belong to the endomembrane

105
00:04:36,325 --> 00:04:37,994
system, because they have membranes.

106
00:04:38,394 --> 00:04:39,535
But they do not.

107
00:04:39,535 --> 00:04:43,531
Their work is not coordinated with
the ER, Golgi complex, lysosomes and

108
00:04:43,531 --> 00:04:44,431
vacuoles.

109
00:04:44,853 --> 00:04:46,141
Where is this asked?

110
00:04:46,141 --> 00:04:50,178
As match or statement questions on
Golgi faces, ER types, lysosome enzymes

111
00:04:50,178 --> 00:04:51,327
and the tonoplast.

112
00:04:51,927 --> 00:04:53,076
Exam level now.

113
00:04:53,076 --> 00:04:55,520
Match each structure with its feature.

114
00:04:55,520 --> 00:04:56,717
Pause and solve.

115
00:05:02,117 --> 00:05:03,624
The answer is D.

116
00:05:03,624 --> 00:05:06,579
One r, two p, three s, four q.

117
00:05:07,079 --> 00:05:10,240
Smooth ER makes lipids, and
steroid hormones in animal cells.

118
00:05:10,240 --> 00:05:12,357
The Golgi cis face is the forming face.

119
00:05:12,857 --> 00:05:14,208
Lysosomes hold hydrolases.

120
00:05:14,208 --> 00:05:16,738
And the tonoplast is the
membrane of the vacuole.

121
00:05:17,338 --> 00:05:18,878
Here is your recap.

122
00:05:18,878 --> 00:05:20,460
Screenshot this card.

123
00:05:23,660 --> 00:05:25,847
Can you answer these three
questions without help?

124
00:05:26,247 --> 00:05:27,147
One.

125
00:05:26,660 --> 00:05:28,621
What is the middle lamella made of?

126
00:05:29,521 --> 00:05:30,421
Two.

127
00:05:29,784 --> 00:05:30,684
Which ER makes lipids?

128
00:05:31,503 --> 00:05:32,403
Three.

129
00:05:32,014 --> 00:05:34,512
What is the membrane
around a vacuole called?

130
00:05:35,612 --> 00:05:40,211
Next time: one organelle burns food
for energy, another catches sunlight.

131
00:05:40,211 --> 00:05:41,111
How?

132
00:05:40,704 --> 00:05:43,332
We find out in mitochondria and plastids.
